<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilocos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ilocos Region</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilocos is a region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, a narrow plain between the Cordillera and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, home of the Ilocano people. Its chief town under Spanish rule was Vigan. It was the scene of the revolts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-silang">Diego Silang</a> and Gabriela Silang in 1762–1763 and of several later risings against Spanish taxes and monopolies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilocos.jpg" alt="Ilocos" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luzviminda7641 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paoay_Church_and_Bell_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was originally inhabited by Negritos as early as 30,000 BCE, who were displaced over time by successive waves of Austronesian migrants arriving along the narrow coast in traditional boats. The first wave, some 10,000 to 7,000 years ago, brought the ancestors of the indigenous groups of the Cordillera Central, and later migrations introduced agriculture. Before colonial rule, the Ilocanos, then known as Samtoy, traded with local and foreign merchants at coastal hubs such as Vigan, Candon and Lingayen, which were visited by Austronesian, Arab, Indian, Chinese and Japanese ships. Gold from the Cordillera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, beeswax and stoneware jars were among the goods exchanged. In the south the polity of Caboloan lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agno-river">Agno River</a> basin.</p><p>A year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> the capital on 24 June 1571, Spanish colonisation expanded to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. Martín de Goiti began the conquest of Pangasinan and founded settlements between 1571 and 1573, and in 1572 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salcedo">Juan de Salcedo</a> landed at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agno-river">Agno River</a> with eight armed boats and 80 men. In 1574 the Chinese pirate Limahong fled to Pangasinan after being driven from Manila, but an army led by Salcedo chased him out after a seven-month siege.</p><p>American forces landed in the region on 18 November 1899 in pursuit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> under Gen. Manuel Tinio kept fighting in the countryside. At the Battle of Tirad Pass on 2 December 1899, Gen. Gregorio del Pilar and about 60 soldiers faced over 300 American troops and lost their position. Japanese forces landed at Vigan on 10 December 1941 and took it the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ilocos Region, designated Region I, lies in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> between the Cordillera and the West Philippine Sea. It has four provinces, Ilocos Norte, Ilocos Sur, La Union and Pangasinan, and the city of San Fernando in La Union is its regional centre. Pangasinan was transferred to it from Central Luzon by a decree of 1973, and Abra, Mountain Province and Benguet were moved to the Cordillera Administrative Region in 1987. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 5,292,297 people, mostly Ilocanos, with Pangasinans the second largest group.</p><p>Ilocano is the main language, and the epic Biag ni Lam-ang belongs to its literary tradition. Two UNESCO World Heritage sites stand here: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> churches of Paoay and Santa Maria, and the colonial city of Vigan. Pangasinan is known for milkfish farming and salt production, and the region's crafts include inabel weaving and damili, red clay pottery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilocos Region” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The occupation encouraged uprisings, notably that of Diego Silang in <b>Ilocos</b>, who sought British support and was assassinated in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-manila">British occupation of Manila</a></li></ul></section>
